\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
On ALFWorld ($N{=}135$), the framework identifies a qualitatively different mechanism: information asymmetry (access to admissible actions, reversible navigation failures) rather than death avoidance (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env}). Neither MATH nor ALFWorld validates DAF quantitatively---true replication requires a second catastrophic-failure environment (e.g., NetHack, Crafter). These cross-domain contrasts demonstrate framework applicability, not universality of the death-dominance finding.

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and correlation structure.}
The $r = 0.97$ correlation is specific to catastrophic death ($P{=}100$); defining failure as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$). As shown in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}, this correlation is largely a mathematical consequence of death dominance (partial $r = 0.02$). Death is effectively the only component generating differential routing signal; the 42.9\% internal death rate reflects 7B-scale hallucination vulnerability, and larger models with lower death rates would reduce DAF.

\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
Death avoidance dominance (78.9\% of non-tie labels, DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$) reframes modality routing as a \emph{safety} problem---closer to a conservative default policy than to cost-quality optimization~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive, cogrouter-2026}. The 86:9 label imbalance explains why always-deterministic is a strong baseline. The capacity scaling results reinforce this: 4B parameters for $+9$\,pp validates our analytical methodology over brute-force learned routing. An untested but predictive ablation: augmenting the internal strategy with state verification (querying the environment to confirm transitions) should drive DAF toward zero, shifting routing signal from failure prevention to other factors---illustrating the framework's diagnostic utility beyond measurement.

\paragraph{Framework applicability.}
The framework applies most directly to environments with sparse terminal rewards and catastrophic failure modes. ScienceWorld satisfies both; MATH satisfies neither, and our framework correctly identifies the absence of death-driven signal there. Environments with graded failure costs would yield intermediate DAF values. The deliberate heterogeneity in deterministic modality definition across our three environments tests whether the framework generalizes, not whether any single modality definition does.

