\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

Our diagnostic framework quantifies what drives routing signal in interactive environments through three components: (i)~episode-level counterfactual labeling that generates routing preferences from paired pure-strategy outcomes (\S\ref{sec:collection}--\ref{sec:labeling}); (ii)~the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF), which decomposes the routing score gap into death-avoidance and non-death components (\S\ref{sec:daf}); and (iii)~the Signal Concentration Index (SCI), which measures whether routing signal is concentrated in a few task types or distributed systematically across the environment (\S\ref{sec:sci}). Episode-level bootstrap resampling provides 95\% confidence intervals for all metrics.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Consider an interactive environment $\mathcal{E}$ with state space $\mathcal{S}$, observation space $\mathcal{O}$, and action space $\mathcal{A}$. An agent follows a policy $\pi: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{A}$ that maps observation histories to actions. At each step $t$, the agent receives observation $o_t$, selects action $a_t = \pi(o_1, \ldots, o_t)$, and the environment transitions to state $s_{t+1}$.

We define a modality set $\mathcal{M} = \{{\tt int}, {\tt det}\}$ governing how the agent processes state transitions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Internal} ($m = {\tt int}$): The agent uses its own generation to predict intermediate observations. The LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions following the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, but prediction errors compound across steps.
    \item \textbf{Deterministic} ($m = {\tt det}$): The agent receives ground-truth observations from the environment simulator at each step---equivalent to access to a perfect dynamics model. Action selection remains with the LLM; only the state-transition prediction is replaced.
\end{itemize}

\noindent We restrict $\mathcal{M}$ to two modalities. An ablation comparing chain-of-thought reasoning (deep internal) against direct-answer generation (fast internal) on 6 overlapping task types ($n{=}120$ episodes per condition) found no significant difference on any task (Mann--Whitney $p > 0.26$, $|d| \leq 0.31$) and near-identical death rates (41.8\% vs.\ 42.5\%), confirming that reasoning depth does not meaningfully alter internal-mode outcomes in this setting.

A \emph{routing policy} $\rho: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{M}$ selects which modality to use at each step. The goal of modality routing is to learn $\rho$ that maximizes expected episode return. Our analysis investigates what signal is available to learn such a router.

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps (across 11 prescreened task types) produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

\paragraph{Pure-strategy trajectories.}
For a task instance $t$ and modality $m \in \mathcal{M}$, a \emph{pure-strategy trajectory} is the complete episode obtained by applying modality $m$ at every step:
\begin{equation}
    \tau_m^{(t)} = (o_1, a_1, o_2, a_2, \ldots, o_T, a_T)
\end{equation}
where $T \leq T_{\max}$ is the episode length (capped at $T_{\max} = 30$ steps). Both trajectories $\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}$ and $\tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}$ are executed under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation), ensuring that performance differences arise solely from the modality.

Each trajectory $\tau$ yields a terminal score $s(\tau) \in \mathbb{Z}$ and a death indicator $d(\tau) \in \{0, 1\}$. When the agent dies ($d = 1$), the environment imposes a penalty: $s(\tau) = -P$ where $P > 0$ ($P = 100$ in ScienceWorld). The episode pair produces a tuple:
\begin{multline}
    \bigl(s^{\tt int}, s^{\tt det}, d^{\tt int}, d^{\tt det}\bigr) \triangleq \\
    \bigl(s(\tau_{\tt int}),\, s(\tau_{\tt det}),\, d(\tau_{\tt int}),\, d(\tau_{\tt det})\bigr)
\end{multline}

We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The framework is environment-agnostic: it requires only paired episode outcomes under two modalities, with no assumptions about score structure or failure mode. We apply it unchanged to ALFWorld and MATH in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{Episode-Level Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

We define a labeling function $L$ that maps a counterfactual pair to a routing preference:
\begin{multline}
    L\!\bigl(\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}, \tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}\bigr) \;\rightarrow \\
    \ell \in \{{\tt det\text{-}pref},\; {\tt int\text{-}pref},\; {\tt no\text{-}pref}\}
\end{multline}

The labeling function is \emph{death-aware}: it prioritizes survival over score, reflecting the asymmetric cost of catastrophic failure in interactive environments. ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. The full procedure is given in Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_overview.pdf}
\caption{Counterfactual episode collection and analysis pipeline. Each task instance is run under both pure strategies independently, producing episode-level labels via Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Episode-Level Counterfactual Labeling}
\label{alg:labeling}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Task instance $t$, modalities $\mathcal{M} = \{{\tt int}, {\tt det}\}$
\ENSURE Label $\ell \in \{{\tt det\text{-}pref}, {\tt int\text{-}pref}, {\tt no\text{-}pref}\}$
\FOR{each $m \in \mathcal{M}$}
    \STATE Run episode $\tau_m$ with pure-strategy $m$
    \STATE Record score $s_m$ and death indicator $d_m$
\ENDFOR
\IF{$d_{\tt int} = 1$ \AND $d_{\tt det} = 0$}
    \STATE $\ell \leftarrow {\tt det\text{-}pref}$ \COMMENT{death asymmetry}
\ELSIF{$d_{\tt int} = 0$ \AND $d_{\tt det} = 1$}
    \STATE $\ell \leftarrow {\tt int\text{-}pref}$ \COMMENT{death asymmetry}
\ELSIF{$d_{\tt int} = d_{\tt det} = 1$}
    \STATE $\ell \leftarrow {\tt no\text{-}pref}$ \COMMENT{both dead}
\ELSIF{$s_{\tt det} > s_{\tt int}$}
    \STATE $\ell \leftarrow {\tt det\text{-}pref}$ \COMMENT{score comparison}
\ELSIF{$s_{\tt int} > s_{\tt det}$}
    \STATE $\ell \leftarrow {\tt int\text{-}pref}$ \COMMENT{score comparison}
\ELSE
    \STATE $\ell \leftarrow {\tt no\text{-}pref}$ \COMMENT{tied alive}
\ENDIF
\RETURN $\ell$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The four mutually exclusive cases correspond to:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry} (lines 5--8): $d^{\tt int} \neq d^{\tt det}$ $\rightarrow$ the surviving strategy is preferred. This accounts for 78.9\% of all non-tie labels (75 of 95).
    \item \textbf{Both dead} (lines 9--10): $d^{\tt int} = d^{\tt det} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ ${\tt no\_preference}$.
    \item \textbf{Score comparison} (lines 11--14): both survive and $s^{\tt det} \neq s^{\tt int}$ $\rightarrow$ the higher-scoring strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive} (lines 15--16): both survive and $s^{\tt det} = s^{\tt int}$ $\rightarrow$ ${\tt no\_preference}$.
\end{enumerate}

Label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we decompose the mean score gap between strategies. Let $\delta = \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt int}] - \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt det}]$ denote the death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ the mean score gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy dies. The full gap decomposes as:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \underbrace{\delta \cdot P}_{\text{death contribution}} + \Delta_{\text{nd}}
\end{equation}
The approximation holds exactly when the surviving strategy scores zero in all death-asymmetry episodes; in our data, the residual is small ($29.60 - 29.37 = 0.23$, or $0.8\%$ of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

We define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) as the fraction of the full score gap attributable to death avoidance:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}} \approx \frac{\delta P}{\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}}
\end{equation}%
\footnote{When $\Delta_{\text{nd}} < 0$ (deterministic strategy scores lower than internal on non-death episodes), DAF exceeds 1, indicating death avoidance overcompensates for a non-death disadvantage.}

DAF is well-defined when $\Delta_{\text{full}} > 0$; in our data, the minimum per-task $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $+9.58$ (\texttt{find-non-living-thing}), ensuring numerical stability. Since DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}})$, it increases monotonically with $P$---a mathematical consequence, not an empirical finding. DAF should therefore be interpreted relative to the environment's penalty magnitude: it quantifies the penalty's amplification of death asymmetry, not a universal constant. We report both DAF at the environment default ($P{=}100$) and the penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction (78.9\%) as complementary metrics.

To assess sensitivity to penalty magnitude, we recalculate DAF under hypothetical death penalties (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}): $P{=}10 \rightarrow$ DAF $= 50.4\%$, $P{=}50 \rightarrow 82.6\%$, $P{=}100 \rightarrow 90.4\%$, $P{=}200 \rightarrow 95.0\%$. Death avoidance remains the dominant component across all penalty magnitudes, though its relative contribution scales with penalty severity. As a penalty-independent companion metric, 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels (75 of 95) are driven by death asymmetry rather than score comparison (\S\ref{sec:labeling}), confirming that death avoidance dominates routing signal regardless of penalty magnitude.

\paragraph{Statistical inference.}
We quantify uncertainty via episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$ resamples). Each resample draws $N = 245$ episodes with replacement, recomputes $\Delta_{\text{full}}$, $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$, and DAF, and derives the percentile-based 95\% CI. The aggregate DAF CI is $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$, excluding both 50\% and 100\%. Per-task bootstrap CIs confirm $\Delta > 0$ for 10 of 11 task types; \texttt{chemistry-mix} ($N{=}20$, $\Delta = 21.8$, CI $[-5.0, 48.6]$) is the sole exception, attributable to small sample size rather than absence of signal.

We resample at the episode level rather than the step level because the episode is the unit of counterfactual comparison: each episode pair yields one label, and steps within an episode are sequentially dependent. Episode-level resampling preserves this correlation structure and matches the granularity at which DAF is defined.

\subsection{Signal Concentration Index}
\label{sec:sci}

DAF quantifies \emph{how much} routing signal derives from death avoidance; a complementary question is whether this signal is driven by a few outlier task types or distributed systematically. We define the Signal Concentration Index (SCI) using the Gini coefficient over per-task routing signal contributions.

Let $K$ denote the number of task types and $c_k$ the contribution of task type $k$ to the aggregate routing signal, defined as the fraction of total death-rate differential attributable to task $k$:
\begin{equation}
    c_k = \frac{n_k \cdot \delta_k}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} n_j \cdot \delta_j}
\end{equation}
where $n_k$ is the number of episodes and $\delta_k = d_k^{\tt int} - d_k^{\tt det}$ is the per-task death-rate differential. The SCI is the Gini coefficient over $\{c_1, \ldots, c_K\}$:
\begin{equation}
    \text{SCI} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^K \sum_{j=1}^K |c_i - c_j|}{2K \sum_{k=1}^K c_k}
\end{equation}

SCI $\in [0, 1]$: a value near 0 indicates uniform distribution of routing signal across task types (systematic phenomenon); a value near 1 indicates concentration in a single task type (isolated phenomenon). In our data, SCI $= 0.2147$, indicating low concentration. The top-1 task type contributes 14.3\% of total signal, top-2 contributes 27.5\%, and top-3 contributes 39.8\%---no single task dominates. Of 11 task types, 10 exhibit nonzero death signal (\texttt{find-non-living-thing} has zero deaths in both modalities). Together with DAF, SCI establishes that death avoidance is both the dominant routing signal (DAF $= 90.4\%$) and a systematic phenomenon distributed across nearly all task types (SCI $= 0.2147$).

